DOMAIN: Marx's Own Texts and the Development of Historical Materialism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-27
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 8, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Historical materialism is Marx's theory that the social forms of
human life---including law, politics, religion, and morality---rise
and fall as they further and then impede the development of human
productive power. The locus classicus is the 1859 Preface to A
Contribution to the Critique of Political Economy, where Marx gives
his most compact and notorious statement: the economic structure of
society (relations of production) constitutes the real foundation on
which rises a legal and political superstructure and to which
correspond definite forms of social consciousness. A standing
interpretive problem is whether this formulation commits Marx to a
strict economic determinism, or whether it is compatible with the
historical-political analysis of class agency displayed in works like
The Eighteenth Brumaire of Louis Bonaparte (1852). The German Ideology
(1845-46, with Engels) is the first systematic statement of the
materialist conception of history, grounding it in the premise of
real, living individuals and their material conditions of existence.

The Grundrisse (1857-58, first published 1939) has become central to
twentieth-century rereadings of Marx, supplying both the methodological
Introduction (with its abstract-to-concrete dialectical method) and the
"Forms which Precede Capitalist Production" section that sketches
alternative paths of social development beyond the European norm. The
late writings---letters to Vera Zasulich (1881), the ethnological
notebooks (1880-81), and correspondence on the Russian commune---
dramatically complicate any linear-stadial reading of historical
materialism by suggesting that non-Western societies might bypass
capitalism to reach communism. G. A. Cohen's Karl Marx's Theory of
History (1978) remains the anchor of the analytical Marxist tradition
and the main target for critics of technological determinism. More
recent scholarship---Kevin Anderson (2010), Marcello Musto (2023),
Michał Siermiński (2025)---pursues a non-deterministic, multilinear
reading of Marx's philosophy of history.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain covers the primary textual record and major interpretive
monographs through which Marx's theory of history has been understood.
It establishes the baseline for tracing how 19th-century and
20th-century interpretations departed from, refined, or distorted
Marx's own formulations, and it maps the internal tensions (1859
Preface vs Eighteenth Brumaire; early vs late Marx; stadial vs
multilinear reading) that drive subsequent interpretive debates.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Scholarly work specifically on the Grundrisse in English is
underrepresented in the available journal literature; the most
important treatments remain embedded in monographs. Comparative
studies of the MEGA2 critical edition's impact on philological
debates about the German Ideology manuscripts are also sparse in
English-language philosophy journals. Three entries are marked
INCOMPLETE (no abstract available): anderson2010marxmargins,
andersonkb2002latewritings, sayercorrigan1987revolution.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The two axes of determinism vs. agency (1859 Preface vs. Eighteenth
Brumaire) and stadial vs. multilinear development (Capital vs. late
writings) should structure the review section on primary texts.
Siermiński's 2025 pair of articles can anchor a periodized narrative
of Marx's intellectual development from the 1840s through the 1850s.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Technological-determinist reading: 4 papers - G. A. Cohen's
  functionalist-determinist reconstruction and its critics
- Agency and conjuncture reading: 3 papers - Eighteenth Brumaire,
  Tomba, Siermiński
- Multilinear/late-Marx reading: 5 papers - ethnological notebooks,
  Anderson, Musto, Sayer/Corrigan, D'Elia/Shanin
- Philological-genetic reading: 2 papers - Omura on German Ideology,
  Ninos on Grundrisse-Hegel connection
====================================================================
}

