DOMAIN: Western Marxism and Hegelian-Humanist Readings
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-28
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 8, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Western Marxism designates a heterodox tradition emerging in the 1920s
that broke with the mechanistic determinism of the Second International
by returning to Hegel and the categories of consciousness, totality,
and praxis. Georg Lukacs's History and Class Consciousness (1923)
and Karl Korsch's Marxism and Philosophy (1923) are its founding
documents. Lukacs recast historical materialism through the Hegelian
concept of totality: capitalism appears as a structured whole in which
reification -- the experience of social relations as thing-like --
pervades all consciousness; the proletariat, as the identical
subject-object of history, is in principle capable of recognizing the
totality and breaking through reification. Korsch, independently,
argued that philosophy could not simply be sublated into science;
it retains a critical function within the class struggle. Gramsci's
Prison Notebooks (written 1929-35) extended this tradition by
replacing the base/superstructure metaphor with hegemony and the
philosophy of praxis: consent, intellectual leadership, and the
formation of collective will in civil society are as fundamental
to historical change as economic contradictions.

The Frankfurt School (Horkheimer, Adorno, Marcuse) deepened the
critique while becoming more pessimistic about the revolutionary
subject. Horkheimer's "Traditional and Critical Theory" (1937)
distinguished critical theory's reflexive, emancipatory aim from
positivist social science. Marcuse's Reason and Revolution (1941)
recovered Hegel for a radical social theory. Adorno's immanent
critique of reification in Negative Dialectics rejected Lukacs's
teleological faith in the proletariat while preserving the category
of totality as negative standard. The recovery of Marx's 1844
Economic and Philosophical Manuscripts in the 1930s fuelled humanist
interpretations emphasizing alienation and species-being. Sartre's
Critique of Dialectical Reason (1960) attempted to found a Marxist
anthropology on existentialist praxis, reconstructing the dialectical
intelligibility of history from the individual act upward through
groups and institutions. Perry Anderson's Considerations on Western
Marxism (1976) provided the classic overview and periodization of
the tradition.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the richest body of interpretation of Marx's
philosophy of history that emphasizes agency, consciousness, and
dialectical method over structural or evolutionary readings. The
concepts of totality, reification, hegemony, and praxis directly
address how history is made rather than merely determined, and how
historical actors can come to understand and transform their
conditions. The tradition also reveals how interpretive choices about
Marx's texts (especially the early vs. mature writings) generate
fundamentally different philosophies of history.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Recent scholarship on Black Marxism and postcolonial critiques of
Western Marxism's Eurocentrism is not well covered here. The
Frankfurt School's relationship to Benjamin and messianic
historiography -- another important strand -- receives only
incidental attention.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The entries fall into three temporal clusters: canonical founding
texts (1920s-1960s), classic secondary surveys (1970s-2000s), and
recent critical reassessments (2008-2024). Synthesis should track
the debate between praxis-centred and structure-centred readings
of totality and trace the reception of Gramsci's hegemony concept
across these generations.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Praxis-totality (Lukacs/Korsch tradition): 5 papers -- history is
  constituted through collective class praxis grasped via the
  category of totality
- Hegemony/philosophy of praxis (Gramscian): 4 papers -- historical
  change requires intellectual-moral leadership and transformation
  of common sense
- Frankfurt School immanent critique: 4 papers -- totality functions
  as negative standard; no privileged revolutionary subject
- Sartrean existential Marxism: 2 papers -- dialectical
  intelligibility grounded in individual praxis scaled to groups
====================================================================
}

