DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The theoretical debates within historical materialism cluster around
five interconnected axes. First, the base/superstructure metaphor:
whether economic relations causally determine legal, political, and
ideological forms (hard determinism), or whether the superstructure
possesses relative autonomy that can react back on the base
(Althusser, Williams, Wood). Second, the "primacy of productive
forces" thesis (G.A. Cohen's 1978 KMTH): that productive forces
explain the character of production relations, and that their
development tendency explains the succession of modes of production.
This thesis provoked a sharp counter from the social relations school
(Brenner, Wood), who argue that class conflict and property relations
cannot be derived from technological imperatives. Third, the
teleology question: does historical materialism presuppose an
immanent directional logic (toward communism) that commits it to
historical inevitability, or can it be reconstructed as a
non-teleological explanatory framework? Cohen's functional
explanation defense and Honderich's critique are the locus classicus
here. Fourth, the agency/structure tension: does historical
materialism subordinate individual and collective agency to structural
determination, or can class struggle be theorized as the engine of
history (Thompson vs Althusser; Callinicos's synthesis)? Fifth, the
unilinear vs multilinear debate: whether all societies pass through
the same sequence of modes of production (the classical "five stages"
doctrine) or whether Marx himself allowed for multiple developmental
paths, as suggested by his late writings on Russia and the Asiatic
mode of production.

The field's central reference point is Cohen (1978), which catalyzed
the "analytical Marxism" program. Brenner's empirical-historical
challenge to Cohen's technological determinism and Wood's political
Marxism represent the main alternative tradition. Recent scholarship
(Chibber 2011) attempts to adjudicate what is viable in Cohen's
framework while incorporating the social relations school's insights.
Carling (1993) provides a comprehensive mapping of the analytical
Marxism/historical materialism debate.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain covers the core interpretive controversies that any
reconstruction of Marx's theory of history must navigate: the nature
of historical causation, the role of agency, and the scope of
historical necessity. The base/superstructure, teleology, and
forces/relations debates directly bear on how competing traditions
(Hegelian, analytical, structuralist, humanist) have read Marx.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The debate on multilinear history and the Asiatic mode of production
is underrepresented in English-language philosophical literature;
most treatments remain primarily historical-sociological rather than
philosophical. Recent Continental reconstructions (Balibar, Negri)
are less well covered by database searches than the analytical
tradition. No entry is included for E.P. Thompson's Poverty of Theory
(the humanist/anti-structuralist pole) since CrossRef could not
verify its metadata.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Structure around the five debate-axes above; treat Cohen (1978) as
the axis of the forces/teleology cluster, and Wood/Brenner as the
axis of the social relations cluster. Callinicos and Chibber provide
bridging positions for the agency/structure debate.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Primacy of Productive Forces (technological determinism): 4 papers -- Cohen KMTH, Cohen functional, Shaw, Carling
- Social Relations School (political Marxism): 4 papers -- Brenner 1976, Brenner 1977, Brenner 1982, Wood
- Relative Autonomy / Western Marxism: 1 paper -- Williams
- Agency and Structure synthesis: 2 papers -- Callinicos, Chibber
- Critical survey: 2 papers -- Honderich, Roberts
====================================================================
}

