DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Contemporary scholarship on Marx's philosophy of history has been
transformed by three overlapping developments since 2005. First, the
ongoing publication of the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe (MEGA2) has made
available previously unpublished or underread manuscripts — including
Marx's late ethnological notebooks, the Grundrisse in new critical
editions, and extensive scientific excerpts — revealing a thinker far
more attentive to natural science, non-Western societies, and
historical plurality than the standard "modes of production" narrative
allows. Musto's work in particular has drawn attention to how MEGA2
complicates teleological readings. Second, ecological Marxism has
reconstituted historical materialism around the concept of "metabolic
rift" — drawn from Marx's Capital and natural-scientific notebooks —
arguing that capitalism structurally disrupts the metabolic exchange
between humanity and nature. Saito's Karl Marx's Ecosocialism (2017)
and Marx in the Anthropocene (2023) represent the most developed
reconstruction, using MEGA2 sources to argue that the late Marx
abandoned productivist assumptions in favor of a "degrowth" vision.
This strand has generated debate: Moore argues the metabolic rift
framework remains too dualistic and proposes a world-ecology approach
that integrates nature into the logic of capital. Third, revisionist
readings of historical materialism's core concepts have proliferated.
Postone's Time, Labor, and Social Domination — published in 1993 but
increasingly influential — reinterprets labor not as the ontological
foundation of social life but as a historically specific form of
social domination under capitalism, dissolving the base/superstructure
model. Tomba's Marx's Temporalities extends this by arguing that
historical materialism operates with plural and non-synchronous
temporal layers rather than a single developmental logic. Banaji's
Theory as History (2010) challenges the standard succession of modes
of production, showing how "free" wage labor and "unfree" labor coexist
within capitalism's historical trajectory.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly covers the most contested contemporary
reinterpretations of Marx's philosophy of history, providing the
contemporary end of any trajectory tracing interpretations from Marx's
own time to the present. The MEGA2 scholarship, ecological readings,
and temporality debates together reframe what "historical materialism"
means as a theory of history, offering the research project its
contemporary landscape.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct engagement with Althusser's influence on current debates is
underrepresented in the verified literature, as is systematic
treatment of how Anglo-American analytic Marxism (Cohen, Elster) now
stands in relation to these newer interpretations. Several core books
(Saito 2017, Postone 1993, Tomba 2013, Banaji 2010) lack
machine-readable abstracts in S2/CrossRef and are marked INCOMPLETE.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Synthesis should contrast the "metabolic rift" strand (Saito, Foster,
Moore) with the "temporalities" strand (Tomba, Postone) and the
"modes of production" revisionism (Banaji, Anderson), showing how
each engages differently with MEGA2's textual resources and with
the Promethean critique of classical historical materialism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Ecological/metabolic rift: 4 papers — Marx's critique of capitalism
  as disruption of humanity-nature metabolism; MEGA2 grounds degrowth reading
- Temporality and abstract domination: 3 papers — plural historical
  temporalities; abstract labor as historically specific social form
- Modes of production revisionism: 3 papers — non-linear succession;
  colonial and racial dimensions of capitalist history
- MEGA2 textual scholarship: 2 papers — new archival findings reshape
  the Marx of the late manuscripts and ethnological notebooks
====================================================================
}

@book{cohen1978marxtheory,
  author = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title = {Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9780691213002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen reconstructs historical materialism as a
  rigorous social scientific theory centered on the Development
  Thesis (productive forces tend to develop over time) and the
  Primacy Thesis (relations of production are explained by the
  level of productive forces they optimally utilize). He defends
  a functionalist explanation of the superstructure's character
  by its tendency to stabilize the economic base.

  RELEVANCE: This is the primary target of both the analytical
  Marxist debate and the post-analytical critique of technological
  determinism. Understanding the theory of historical materialism
  in the 20th century cannot proceed without it; the Siermiński
  2025 papers explicitly position themselves against Cohen's
  technologically determinist reading of the 1840s Marx.

  POSITION: Analytical Marxist, technological-determinist
  reconstruction of historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, analytical-marxism, productive-forces, determinism, High},
  abstract = {First published in 1978, this book rapidly established itself as a classic of modern Marxism. In this expanded edition Cohen offers his own account of the history, and the further promise, of analytical Marxism. He also expresses reservations about traditional historical materialism, in the light of which he reconstructs the theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{leopold2007youngmarx,
  author = {Leopold, David},
  title = {The Young Karl Marx: German Philosophy, Modern Politics and Human Flourishing},
  year = {2007},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9780511490606},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Leopold offers a close philosophical reading of
  Marx's early writings through 1845, arguing that the young
  Marx's thought is best understood as a distinctive fusion of
  Hegelian philosophy, left-Hegelian social critique, and a
  normative concern with human flourishing. The book resists
  the Althusserian "epistemological break" and the humanist
  reading that flattens the early texts into a simple
  pre-scientific humanism.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the philosophical starting point from
  which historical materialism develops; the 1844 Manuscripts
  and Theses on Feuerbach receive sustained analysis that
  grounds assessment of how the theory of history changes from
  early to mature Marx.

  POSITION: Post-Althusserian, philosophically rigorous reading
  of early Marx.
  },
  keywords = {early-marx, german-ideology, 1844-manuscripts, Feuerbach, human-flourishing, High},
  abstract = {The Young Karl Marx is an innovative and important study of Marx's early writings. These writings provide the fascinating spectacle of a powerful and imaginative intellect wrestling with complex and significant issues, but they also present formidable interpretative obstacles to modern readers. David Leopold shows how an understanding of their intellectual and cultural context can illuminate the political dimension of these works. An erudite yet accessible discussion of Marx's influences and targets frames the author's critical engagement with Marx's account of the emergence, character, and (future) replacement of the modern state. This combination of historical and analytical approaches results in a sympathetic, but not uncritical, exploration of such fundamental themes as alienation, citizenship, community, anti-semitism, and utopianism. The Young Karl Marx is a scholarly and original work which provides a radical and persuasive reinterpretation of Marx's complex and often misunderstood views of German philosophy, modern politics, and human flourishing.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{stedmanjones2018marx,
  author = {Stedman Jones, Gareth},
  title = {Karl Marx's Changing Picture of the End of Capitalism},
  journal = {Journal of the British Academy},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {6},
  pages = {187--206},
  doi = {10.5871/jba/006.187},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stedman Jones traces three successive attempts
  by Marx to theorise the value form and the end of capitalism:
  a Feuerbachian-humanist model in the 1840s; a Hegelian-logical
  model in the Grundrisse following the 1857 crisis; and the more
  historically nuanced model of Capital (1867), accompanied by a
  revised conception of revolutionary transition as a process
  rather than an event. The "Marxism" of the Second International
  emerges after Marx's death from a different intellectual context.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a periodized intellectual biography of
  Marx's philosophy of history keyed to his three major
  formulations, from the early writings through the Grundrisse
  to Capital. It directly addresses how to read the 1859 Preface
  and the Grundrisse in relation to Capital.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical, critical biographical
  reading.
  },
  keywords = {marx-development, grundrisse, capital, value-form, 1859-preface, High},
  abstract = {This essay examines three successive attempts Marx made to theorise his conception of the ‘value form’ or the capitalist mode of production. The first in the 1840s ascribed the destruction of an original human sociability to the institution of private property and looked forward to its destruction and transcendence in the coming revolution. This vision was shattered by the disenchanting failure of the 1848 revolutions. The second attempt, belonging to the 1850s and outlined in the Grundrisse, attempted to chart the rise, global triumph, and the ultimate destruction of what Marx called the ‘value form’. Its model of global triumph and final disintegration was inspired by Hegel’s Logic. But the global economic crisis of 1857–8 did not lead to the return of revolution. Marx’s disturbed reaction to this failure was seen in his paranoia about the failure of his Critique of Political Economy (1859). Marx’s third attempt to formulate his critique in Das Kapital in 1867 was much more successful. It was accompanied by a new conception of revolution as a transi­ tional process rather than an event and was stimulated by his participation in the International Working Men’s Association and the accompanying growth of cooper­ atives, trade unions, and a political reform movement culminating in the Reform Bill of 1867. This multifaceted picture of transition took the place of the neo­Jacobin conception of revolution dominant in 1848. In the 1870s, this optimistic sense of development was halted by the Franco­ Prussian War, the Commune, trade union moves towards the Gladstone Liberal Pact, and the increasing repression of the Social Democrats in Germany. In this context, especially after Marx’s death, transition to socialism came to be considered the result of the collapse of capitalism rather than the political activity of progressive parties. This was the context in which so called ‘Marxism’ was born.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{sierminskia2025youngmarx,
  author = {Siermiński, Michał},
  title = {Young Karl Marx in the Age of Progress: The Historical Materialism of the 1840s and Its Limits},
  journal = {ORGANON},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {57},
  pages = {159},
  doi = {10.4467/00786500.org.25.009.22613},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The article argues that the young Marx's
  historical materialism rests on two premises---the Development
  Thesis (history is continuous development of productive forces)
  and the Primacy Thesis (productive forces determine social
  relations)---both of which generate difficulties for Marx's
  own growing enthusiasm for workers' class struggle, since a
  technologically determinist framework leaves little room for
  political agency.

  RELEVANCE: Gives the most analytically precise recent account
  of the early Marx's historical materialism and its internal
  tensions, directly engaging with the Development/Primacy
  thesis framework from Cohen and setting up the companion paper
  on the mature Marx's break from these premises.

  POSITION: Critical internal analysis of early-Marx historical
  materialism; identifies the limits of the 1840s formulation.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, early-marx, development-thesis, primacy-thesis, determinism, High},
  abstract = {The article discusses an early version of Karl Marx’s historical materialism. The young Marx was a child of his time—the era of rapid development of technology and science that accompanied the expansion of capitalism. In the 1840s his historical materialism was based on two premises: the Development Thesis (history is the continuous development of the productive forces) and the Primacy Thesis (the level of development of the productive forces determines social relations). On the basis of these assumptions, Marx had serious problems in theoretically grounding his growing enthusiasm for the workers’ class struggle.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{sierminskib2025maturemarx,
  author = {Siermiński, Michał},
  title = {Marx Against Marx: An Outline of Mature Historical Materialism},
  journal = {ORGANON},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {57},
  pages = {177},
  doi = {10.4467/00786500.org.25.010.22614},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tracing the intellectual revolution of the
  1850s---the methodological turn in the Grundrisse and its
  anti-positivist stance---the article argues that Marx's
  mature theory accords primacy to relations of production over
  productive forces, breaking with early technological
  determinism. Surplus value and the sphere of production
  (not market appearances) become explanatory anchors.

  RELEVANCE: Completes a paired reconstruction of Marx's
  intellectual development showing an internal break within
  historical materialism between 1840s and 1850s, bearing
  directly on how to read the 1859 Preface in relation to the
  Grundrisse and Capital.

  POSITION: Positions the mature Marx as a non-determinist
  whose theory of production-relations primacy overrides
  the early forces-of-production primacy.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, grundrisse, relations-of-production, mature-marx, 1859-preface, High},
  abstract = {In the 1850s, Karl Marx’s thought underwent a genuine revolution. After relocating to England, he confronted classical political economy anew, finding it unable to account for value growth and capitalist accumulation. Beginning with the methodological turn in the Grundrisse and its explicitly anti-positivist stance, Marx shifted explanation from market appearances to the sphere of production, where surplus value becomes fundamental. This reorientation culminated in a decisive break with his early technolgical determinism: Marx accords primacy to the rela tions of production over productive forces in explaining the origins of capitalism, the dynamics of accumulation and the specifically capitalist drive toward productivity growth.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{tomba2013eighteenthbrumaire,
  author = {Tomba, Massimiliano},
  title = {Marx as the Historical Materialist: Re-reading The Eighteenth Brumaire},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {2},
  pages = {21--46},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341290},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tomba argues that The Eighteenth Brumaire of
  Louis Bonaparte stages historical materialism as an
  interpretive practice rather than a predictive schema: Marx
  shows how human beings make their own history but under
  inherited conditions, and the text models a form of historical
  analysis in which contingency, class struggle, and
  conjuncture are irreducible. The 1852 text is presented as
  a corrective to any deterministic reading of the 1859 Preface.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the canonical tension between the 1859
  Preface's base-superstructure schema and the Eighteenth
  Brumaire's emphasis on agency, ideology, and political
  contingency. It shows what "historical materialist method"
  looks like in practice in Marx's own political writing.

  POSITION: Anti-determinist reading of the Eighteenth Brumaire
  as the model for genuine historical-materialist analysis.
  },
  keywords = {eighteenth-brumaire, historical-materialism, agency, class-struggle, 1859-preface, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract The purpose of this paper is to re-read Marx’s Eighteenth Brumaire by highlighting the political meaning of a materialist historiography. In the first part, I consider Marx’s historiographical and political intention to represent the history of the aftermath of the revolution of ’48 as a farce in order to liquidate ‘any faith in the superstitious past’. In the second part I analyse the theatrical register chosen by Marx in order to represent the Second Empire as a society without a body, a phantasmagoria in which the Constitution, the National Assembly and law – in short, everything that the middle class had put up as essential principles of modern democracy – disappear. In the third part I argue that Marx does not elaborate a theory of revolution that is good for every occasion. What interests him is a historiography capable of grasping, in the various temporalities of the revolution, the chance for a true liberation.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{musto2023ethnological,
  author = {Musto, Marcello},
  title = {A Reappraisal of Marx's Ethnological Notebooks: Family, Gender, Individual vs. State, and Colonialism},
  journal = {Inter-Asia Cultural Studies},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {24},
  number = {1},
  pages = {117--128},
  doi = {10.1080/14649373.2023.2156124},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Musto reconstructs Marx's reading of Lewis
  Morgan's Ancient Society (1877) and related texts during
  1880-81, arguing that the Ethnological Notebooks show Marx
  at his most critical of the nuclear family, of colonialism,
  and of the progressive narrative of the state. They represent
  a late theoretical preoccupation with alternative,
  communal social forms not reducible to European capitalist
  development.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an authoritative recent reading of the
  Ethnological Notebooks as evidence for the "late Marx"
  revision of his philosophy of history, bearing on whether
  the mature theory remained committed to a European stadial
  sequence.

  POSITION: Late-Marx, anti-Eurocentric, multilinear
  interpretation of the ethnological notebooks.
  },
  keywords = {ethnological-notebooks, late-marx, colonialism, Morgan, gender, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT Between December 1880 and June 1881, Marx’s research interests focused on a new discipline: anthropology. He began with the study of Ancient Society (1877), a work by the U.S. anthropologist Lewis Morgan. What struck Marx most was the way in which Morgan treated production and technological factors as preconditions of social progress, and he felt moved to assemble a compilation of a hundred densely packed pages of excerpts from this book. These make up the bulk of what are known as The Ethnological Notebooks. They also contain excerpts from other works: Java, or How to Manage a Colony (1861) by James Money (1818–1890), a lawyer and Indonesia expert; The Aryan Village in India and Ceylon (1880) by John Phear (1825–1905), president of the supreme court of Ceylon; and Lectures on the Early History of Institutions (1875) by the historian Henry Maine (1822–1888), amounting to a total of another hundred sheets. Marx’s comparative assessments of these authors are fundamental to have a clear idea of the main theoretical preoccupations of the “late Marx” and suggest an innovative reassessment of some of his key concepts.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{andersonkb2002latewritings,
  author = {Anderson, Kevin B.},
  title = {Marx's Late Writings on Non-Western and Precapitalist Societies and Gender},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2002},
  volume = {14},
  number = {4},
  pages = {84--96},
  doi = {10.1080/008935690212331340971},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Anderson surveys Marx's writings from the
  1870s and 1880s---the Zasulich drafts, the ethnological
  notebooks, and correspondence on non-Western societies---to
  show that by this period Marx had abandoned the unilinear
  stadial sequence implicit in the 1859 Preface in favour of
  a genuinely multilinear theory of global social development.
  Gender and precapitalist social forms receive renewed critical
  attention in these late texts.

  RELEVANCE: An important precursor to Anderson's 2010
  monograph, this article isolates the "late Marx" as a
  distinct interpretive problem and argues for its significance
  for reinterpreting the overall philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Multilinear, anti-Eurocentric reading focused on
  the late texts as a revision of the philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {late-marx, ethnological-notebooks, Zasulich, non-Western, multilinear, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{delia2019latemarx,
  author = {D'Elia, Nicola},
  title = {Notes on the Reprint of Late Marx and the Russian Road Edited by Teodor Shanin},
  journal = {International Critical Thought},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {9},
  number = {4},
  pages = {599--609},
  doi = {10.1080/21598282.2019.1695531},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: D'Elia assesses the impact of Shanin's 1983
  edited volume on Marx scholarship, explaining how it changed
  the reading of Marx's Russian correspondence and ethnological
  notebooks. He argues that the volume showed Marx's late
  rethinking extended beyond Russia to a broader reconsideration
  of the political forms suitable for labor's emancipation from
  capitalism, not merely a concession to Russian conditions.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a scholarly retrospective on the Shanin
  collection, which is the key primary-source compilation for
  the "late Marx" debate, and reconstructs the interpretive
  stakes of that debate for the philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Mediating, synthetic assessment of the Shanin
  volume's contribution and its critics.
  },
  keywords = {late-marx, Russia, Shanin, Zasulich, Russian-commune, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT The reprint of Late Marx and the Russian Road on the 200th anniversary of Karl Marx’s birth offers a valuable opportunity to discuss the impact of this path-breaking miscellaneous volume on Marx scholarship. The first aim of this paper is to highlight the original approach of the work, which looked at Marx’s views on Russia from a different perspective to those of previous studies. This paper then deals with the main criticism raised by critics regarding the line of argument advanced in the book. Emphasis is placed on two aspects: (1) the debate over whether Marx believed in the possibility of socialist development in Russia without a proletarian revolution in the Western countries; (2) the argument that the shifts in Marx’s perspective after 1870 were not limited to his writings on the Russian question, which were part of a wider rethink of the political forms suitable for the emancipation of labour from capitalist exploitation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{shanin1983latemarx,
  author = {Shanin, Teodor},
  title = {Late {Marx} and the Russian ``Periphery of Capitalism''},
  journal = {Monthly Review},
  year = {1983},
  doi = {10.14452/mr-035-02-1983-06_2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Shanin introduces the interpretive framework
  of "late Marx" as a distinct phase in which Marx reconsidered
  the peripheries of capitalism. The article presents Marx's
  letters to Vera Zasulich and the drafts of those letters as
  evidence that the late Marx held open the possibility of
  non-capitalist paths to socialism for peasant societies like
  Russia, complicating the dominant reading of historical
  materialism as a unilinear European sequence.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text of the "late Marx"
  thesis that has generated sustained debate about how
  multilinear Marx's philosophy of history really was, and
  whether the late writings require a fundamental revision of
  the standard 1859 Preface interpretation.

  POSITION: Late-Marx thesis; the ur-text of the multilinear
  reading.
  },
  keywords = {late-marx, Russia, Zasulich, multilinear, periphery, High},
  abstract = {Much has been written in the last decades about the differences (or lack of them) between the young and the mature Marx. On the other hand, study of the development of Marx's thought during the last decade of his life has been neglected. This holds true also for the interpretation of his long public silence of those days, unless the often repeated comment of Mehring about Marx's "slow death" is accepted as good enough, even though it flies in the face of the evidence discovered since Mehring's time, i.e., more than thirty thousand pages of notes, extensive commentaries, etc. This is unfortunate, for it was during this period, following the publication in 1867 of the first volume of Capital, that we find major evidence of how little its author was constrained by his own formulas as well as of new directions in which his views changed in response to new conditions and ongoing study.This article can also be found at the Monthly Review website, where most recent articles are published in full.Click here to purchase a PDF version of this article at the Monthly Review website.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{omura2018feuerbach,
  author = {Omura, Izumi},
  title = {Re-examining the Authorship of the Feuerbach Chapter in The German Ideology on the Basis of a Hypothesis of Dictation},
  journal = {The European Journal of the History of Economic Thought},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {25},
  number = {5},
  pages = {808--832},
  doi = {10.1080/09672567.2018.1523940},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Through philological analysis of the
  Feuerbach manuscripts in MEGA2, Omura proposes that most of
  the German Ideology's "Feuerbach" section was dictated by
  Marx and written in Engels's hand, resolving the longstanding
  puzzle of why Engels's handwriting dominates yet Engels
  consistently credited Marx as the originator of the
  materialist conception of history.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses the philological status of the German
  Ideology as a source for historical materialism; the question
  of the text's authorship and compositional history directly
  affects how we read the canonical formulation of the
  materialist conception as a shared vs. individual theoretical
  achievement.

  POSITION: Philological-genetic approach using MEGA2 to
  resolve the authorship question.
  },
  keywords = {german-ideology, Feuerbach, MEGA2, authorship, philology, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract In the Feuerbach manuscripts, the core theses of the materialist conception of history were documented for the first time. Most of the handwriting of the manuscripts belongs to Engels. But later Engels repeatedly stated that the first discoverer of this conception is Marx, not him. This is a contradiction. This contradiction had been discussed for nearly a century, but it has not led scholars to a common result. Why? In my opinion, it was because no one, including the former and new MEGA editors, ever attempted to examine a very important problem, namely, the possibility that the Feuerbach manuscripts could have been dictated by Marx and written by Engels. If this possibility existed really, no contradiction will remain.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2017antiduhring,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Practical Materialism: {Engels's} \textit{Anti-D{\"u}hring} as Marxist Philosophy},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {45},
  number = {4},
  pages = {483--499},
  doi = {10.1080/03017605.2017.1377927},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge argues that Anti-Duhring -- as the only systematic presentation of Marxism endorsed by both Marx and Engels -- has been treated unfairly by critics who caricature it as mechanical materialism. Re-reading Anti-Duhring as a response to the reformist tendencies emerging in 1870s European socialism, he shows that Engels's "practical materialism" contains substantial anti-reductionist and politically engaged dimensions that the standard distortion thesis ignores.

  RELEVANCE: Directly takes up the question of what Engels's most systematic work actually argues about historical materialism, positioning it against both the orthodox codifications of the Second International and the subsequent Althusserian-influenced critiques. Shows the political context of Engels's systematic ambitions.

  POSITION: Defends Engels's mature philosophy against caricatured criticism, arguing for continuity with Marx's historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, Anti-Duhring, historical-materialism, Second-International, orthodox-Marxism, High},
  abstract = {Frederick Engels’s Anti-Dühring was the most important theoretical response to the emerging reformist tendencies within European socialism in the 19th century. It also proved to be Engels’s most influential, and controversial work. Because it is, as Hal Draper points out, ‘the only more or less systematic presentation of Marxism’ by either by Marx or Engels, anyone wanting to reinterpret Marx must first detach it from his seal of approval. It is thus around Anti-Dühring and related texts that debates about the relationship of Marx to ‘Engelsian’ Marxism have tended to focus. This essay re-engages with debates about Engels’s mature work with a view to unpicking his contribution to Marxism from caricatured criticisms of his thought.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ninos2024hegelmarx,
  author = {Ninos, Giannis},
  title = {Hegel's Theory of Finite Cognition and Marx's Critique of Political Economy},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {46},
  number = {3},
  pages = {489--514},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.22},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ninos argues that Hegel's distinction in the
  Science of Logic between analytic and synthetic modes of
  finite cognition provides the methodological framework through
  which Marx, in the Grundrisse, delineates and critiques the
  limitations of classical political economists (especially
  Ricardo). Marx's method in Capital is construed as modeled on
  Hegel's immanent critique of finite cognition from the
  standpoint of absolute cognition.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the Hegel-Marx relationship at the
  methodological level of the Grundrisse, which is essential for
  understanding how the abstract-to-concrete method of the 1857
  Introduction connects to the mature philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Hegelian-methodological reading of the Grundrisse
  and Capital.
  },
  keywords = {grundrisse, Hegel, methodology, political-economy, finite-cognition, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract The article examines the role of Hegel's theory of finite cognition in Marx's critique of classical political economy. I argue that Hegel's distinction of finite cognition between analytic and synthetic in the Science of Logic constitutes the methodological framework through which Marx delineates the different stages of the development of political economy. Focusing on the Grundrisse, I reveal the Hegelian influence behind Marx's statements on previous political economists’ methods. Thus, Marx's immanent critique of the classical political economy is construed as undertaken from the level of the systematic dialectical method of Capital, and is modelled on Hegel's immanent critique of analytic and synthetic cognition from the perspective of absolute cognition. In this context, and focusing on Marx's critique of Ricardo's method, I argue that the latter's limitations are associated with the deficiencies of synthetic cognition as presented by Hegel. Therefore, the article sheds light on an important yet underexplored topic of the Hegel-Marx relationship. By indicating the centrality of Hegel's theory of finite cognition in Marx's methodological underpinnings, the article provides a new perspective on Hegel-Marx scholarship.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{sayercorrigan1987revolution,
  author = {Sayer, Derek and Corrigan, Philip},
  title = {Revolution against the State: The Context and Significance of Marx's Later Writings},
  journal = {Dialectical Anthropology},
  year = {1987},
  volume = {12},
  number = {1},
  pages = {65--82},
  doi = {10.1007/bf00734789},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sayer and Corrigan situate Marx's late writings
  on Russia and pre-capitalist societies in the context of his
  political commitments in the 1870s-80s, arguing that these
  texts represent not a theoretical revision of historical
  materialism but a politically motivated opening toward
  non-Western social movements. The late texts challenge any
  reading that makes Capital's account of capitalist development
  a universal historical template.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the political-contextual reading of the
  late writings that complements the more theory-focused
  accounts of Anderson and Musto. It is an early influential
  statement of the "late Marx" as politically conditioned.

  POSITION: Historical-contextual reading; late writings as
  politically motivated revision rather than systematic
  theoretical shift.
  },
  keywords = {late-marx, Russia, revolution, non-Western, political-context, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{kangal2019intentions,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Engels' Intentions in Dialectics of Nature},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {83},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--243},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2019.83.2.215},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kangal argues that the standard binary framing of the "Engels debate" -- Engels as either contaminator of Marx's materialism or self-started genius of dialectical materialism -- rests on a failure to distinguish authorial, textual, editorial, and interpretational layers of Dialectics of Nature. Applying historical hermeneutics, he shows that Engels's dialectics is less complete than defenders claim but more defensible than critics allege; the meaning of "dialectics" must be located in its specific philosophical and political context rather than projected backward from later Soviet codifications.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the question of how Engels systematized (or failed to systematize) Marx's theory of nature and history, and whether the resulting "dialectical materialism" represents a legitimate extension or a distortion. Central to evaluating whether Second International orthodoxy accurately inherited Engels's own positions.

  POSITION: Rehabilitation of Engels against reductionist readings, via hermeneutic analysis of Dialectics of Nature as an incomplete and contextually specific text.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, dialectics-of-nature, dialectical-materialism, Second-International, hermeneutics, High},
  abstract = {Reading different or controversial intentions into Marx and Engels’ works has been somewhat a common but rather unquestioned practice in the history of Marxist scholarship. Engels’ Dialectics of Nature, a torso for some and a great book for others, is a case in point. A bold line seems to shape the entire Engels debate and separate two opposite views in this regard: Engels the contaminator of Marx's materialism vs. Engels the self-started genius of dialectical materialism. What Engels, unlike Marx, has not enjoyed so far is a critical reflection upon the relationship between different layers of this text: authorial, textual, editorial and interpretational. Informed by a historical hermeneutic, inquiry into the elements that structure the debate on “Dialectics of Nature,” and into the different political and philosophical functions attached to it, makes it possible to relocate the meaning of “dialectics” in a more precise context. Engels’ dialectics is less complete than we usually think it is, but he achieved more than most scholars would like to admit.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{kangal2020book,
  author = {Kangal, Kaan},
  title = {Friedrich Engels and the Dialectics of Nature},
  year = {2020},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  series = {Marx, Engels, and Marxisms},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-34335-4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The first book-length study in English devoted to Engels's Dialectics of Nature, Kangal argues that the text has been systematically misread by both dismissive critics (who find it crudely positivist) and enthusiastic defenders (who project a finished system onto an unfinished manuscript). By reading Dialectics of Nature in relation to Engels's philosophical education and the science of his time, Kangal recovers a more nuanced picture of Engels's naturalism and its relationship to Marx's historical materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained recent scholarly treatment of the text most often cited in debates about whether Engels distorted Marx's philosophy of history by extending dialectics from society to nature. Essential background for evaluating the "Engels problem" in the reception history of historical materialism.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Engels's project, challenging both the Althusserian dismissal and hagiographic readings.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, dialectics-of-nature, historical-materialism, Marx-Engels-relationship, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{blackledge2025crimean,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Friedrich {Engels}, Historical Materialism and the {Crimean War}},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
