  },
  keywords = {Engels, historical-materialism, military-writings, economic-determinism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{morfino2008spinoza,
  author = {Morfino, Vittorio},
  title = {Causa sui or {Wechselwirkung}: {Engels} between {Spinoza} and {Hegel}},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2008},
  volume = {16},
  number = {1},
  pages = {9--35},
  doi = {10.1163/156920608x276279},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Morfino argues that Engels's mature ontology oscillates between two philosophical paradigms: the Spinozist causa sui (self-causing substance) and the Hegelian Wechselwirkung (reciprocal causation). This oscillation generates the internal tensions in Engels's dialectical materialism -- between substance monism and relational determinism -- that shaped subsequent readings of historical materialism in the Second International and beyond.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the philosophical sources of Engels's systematization and why it produced both deterministic and anti-determinist readings. The Spinoza-Hegel tension directly bears on whether Engels's codification introduced teleology into historical materialism.

  POSITION: Immanent philosophical critique, situating Engels's systematic ambitions within the history of European philosophy rather than treating them as simply derivative of Marx.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, Spinoza, Hegel, ontology, dialectical-materialism, Wechselwirkung, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract The essay takes its point of departure from Monod's reading of dialectical materialism in Chance and Necessity. A passage of Engels's Dialectics of Nature, which identifies Spinoza's concept of causa sui with the Hegelian concept of interaction [Wechselwirkung], provides the opportunity to examine the consequences of Monod's claims more closely. Using Spinoza's philosophy as a litmus test, the essay attempts to demonstrate the debt of Engels's materialism to Hegel's Science of Logic by tracing the development of the concept of Wechselwirkung in classical German philosophy. A profound difference between the Spinozan and Hegelian concepts becomes apparent: while the concept of Wechselwirkung implies a totality present to itself as simultaneity, permitting the flow of a linear, homogenous and empty time upon which stages of development can be inscribed, the concept of causa sui implies a totality without closure, a totality whose eternity is identified with the necessary and infinite network of modal durations. The essay concludes by suggesting that Spinoza's concept of causa sui allows us to rethink the relation between freedom and necessity in the Marxist tradition in conjunctural and aleatory terms.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mendelson1979metaphysical,
  author = {Mendelson, Jack},
  title = {On {Engels'} Metaphysical Dialectics: A Foundation of Orthodox {``Marxism''}},
  journal = {Dialectical Anthropology},
  year = {1979},
  volume = {4},
  number = {1},
  pages = {65--73},
  doi = {10.1007/BF00417685},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mendelson argues that Engels's dialectics are "metaphysical" in a technical sense: they impose on history a set of ontological laws (unity of opposites, negation of the negation, transformation of quantity into quality) derived from a generalized philosophy of nature rather than immanent to social-historical analysis. This metaphysical structure became the foundation for the "orthodox Marxism" of Kautsky and Plekhanov, who systematized these laws into a science applicable to all reality.

  RELEVANCE: Articulates the classic "distortion thesis" -- that Engels introduced a metaphysical superstructure alien to Marx's historically immanent method -- and traces its effects on Second International orthodoxy. Provides conceptual vocabulary for the debate about whether orthodox Marxism is a legitimate heir to Marx.

  POSITION: Critical-distortion thesis: Engels's dialectics underlie the rigid determinism of orthodox Second International Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, dialectics, orthodox-Marxism, Kautsky, Plekhanov, metaphysics, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{colletti1971second,
  author = {Colletti, L.},
  title = {The Marxism of the {Second International}},
  journal = {Telos},
  year = {1971},
  volume = {1971},
  number = {8},
  pages = {84--91},
  doi = {10.3817/0671008084},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Colletti argues that the Second International's Marxism -- shaped primarily by Kautsky and Bernstein -- represented a Darwinian-naturalist transformation of Marx's dialectical method. By assimilating historical materialism to natural science (evolutionary gradualism, universal laws of motion), it erased the specifically Hegelian-dialectical elements of Marx's critique and produced both the deterministic orthodoxy and the reformist revisionism it claimed to oppose.

  RELEVANCE: Offers an influential diagnosis of what went wrong in the transition from Marx to orthodox Marxism, identifying the naturalization of history as the central distortion. Frames the Bernstein-Kautsky debate as two variants of the same naturalist paradigm rather than opposites.

  POSITION: Italian Marxist critique: both orthodox Kautskyism and Bernstein's revisionism share a common naturalist-evolutionist epistemology alien to Marx.
  },
  keywords = {Second-International, Kautsky, Bernstein, naturalism, historical-materialism, neo-Kantianism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract The very notion of “Marxism of the Second International” is a loose one which requires precise definition. It will be used here in a limited sense, i.e., as the Marxism of the two major Marxist theoreticians of the period: Kautsky and Plekhanov. This “practical” definition should not be taken to mean that the Marxism of the Second International is a self-contained phenomenon clearly defined with respect to the First International and to what followed. Actually, much of the Marxism of the Second International can be found in Engels' last works: Ludwig Feuerbach; The Origins of the Family, Private Property and the State; and the 1895 “Introduction” to the new edition of Class-Struggles in France.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{gronow2016kautsky,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of {Marxism}: {Karl Kautsky's} Theory of Capitalism, the Marxism of the Second International and {Karl Marx's} Critique of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {Brill},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gronow offers a systematic comparison of Kautsky's theoretical Marxism with Marx's own critique of political economy. He argues that Kautsky's interpretation of capitalism -- organized around theories of immiseration, monopoly formation, and imperialism -- departed significantly from Marx's immanent critique by importing positivist assumptions about economic laws and evolutionary necessity, producing a "scientific socialism" grounded in Darwinian analogies rather than dialectical analysis.

  RELEVANCE: The most thorough recent scholarly treatment of Kautsky's Marxism and its relationship to Marx, providing detailed evidence for how "orthodox" historical materialism transformed Marx's theoretical framework through the specific problems Kautsky addressed (revisionism dispute, imperialism, parliamentary democracy).

  POSITION: Historical-scholarly reconstruction of Kautsky's Marxism, emphasizing his departures from Marx rather than continuities.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Second-International, historical-materialism, political-economy, orthodox-Marxism, High},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{steger1997engels,
  author = {Steger, Manfred B.},
  title = {Friedrich {Engels} and the Origins of {German} Revisionism: Another Look},
  journal = {Political Studies},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {45},
  number = {2},
  pages = {247--259},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-9248.00079},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Steger argues that Engels himself, in his late correspondence and revised introductions to Marx's works, contributed to the ambiguity that enabled Bernstein's revisionism. By emphasizing evolutionary and parliamentarian elements in the 1895 introduction to The Class Struggles in France, Engels opened interpretive space that Bernstein exploited. The origins of German revisionism thus lie partly in Engels's own strategic recalibrations, not only in Bernstein's neo-Kantian innovation.

  RELEVANCE: Complicates the standard narrative of Bernstein as a purely exogenous challenger to Engelsian orthodoxy by tracing revisionism's roots to textual ambiguities in Engels's late work. Important for understanding how Engels's codification generated both orthodoxy and the revisionism it provoked.

  POSITION: Traces the origins of revisionism to contradictions within Engels's own late writings, challenging the sharp Engels/Bernstein distinction.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, Bernstein, revisionism, German-social-democracy, Second-International, High},
  abstract = {The precise nature of Friedrich Engels' theoretical role following Marx's death in 1883 has remained a hotly disputed topic among historians of socialist thought. However, despite their intellectual disagreements, the exponents of various perspectives frequently share a serious interpretive deficiency: the failure to anchor their readings of crucial texts in an analysis of specific political contexts. Situating Engels' later writings – his 1890s Letters on Historical Materialism, and his 1895 Introduction to Marx's The Class Struggles in France – within the dramatically changed political situation in fin de siècle Germany, this essay seeks to recast the entire gestalt of the quarrel over Engels' alleged revisionism by illuminating the historical and political framework that shaped his late Marxism and its theoretical premises.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{steger1992ethics,
  author = {Steger, Manfred},
  title = {Historical Materialism and Ethics: {Eduard Bernstein's} Revisionist Perspective},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {1992},
  volume = {14},
  number = {5},
  pages = {647--663},
  doi = {10.1016/0191-6599(92)90002-T},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Steger analyzes Bernstein's revisionism as a systematic attempt to reconcile historical materialism with neo-Kantian ethics, arguing that Bernstein rejected the deterministic reading of historical materialism (the inevitability of socialist collapse) on empirical grounds and sought to replace it with an ethical socialism in which moral ideals play an autonomous causal role in history alongside economic forces.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a detailed philosophical account of how Bernstein's challenge engaged the philosophical foundations of historical materialism -- specifically the question of whether historical development is law-governed and deterministic, or open to ethical intervention. Directly relevant to evaluating orthodox historical materialism's claims about the direction and necessity of historical change.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction of Bernstein's philosophical revisionism as a principled response to the determinism of Second International orthodoxy.
  },
  keywords = {Bernstein, revisionism, neo-Kantianism, ethics, historical-materialism, Second-International, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{tudor1988revisionism,
  author = {Tudor, H. and Tudor, J. M.},
  title = {Marxism and Social Democracy: The Revisionist Debate 1896--1898},
  year = {1988},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This anthology collects the central texts of the Bernstein-Kautsky revisionism controversy (1896-1898), including Bernstein's original articles in Die Neue Zeit and responses from Kautsky, Parvus, Luxemburg, and others. The editors' introduction situates the debate within the broader context of Second International socialist theory and the theoretical stakes of historical materialism's determinism, showing how the dispute crystallized around the empirical question of capitalist tendencies and the theoretical question of socialist inevitability.

  RELEVANCE: The essential primary-source collection for the revisionism debate, providing the actual texts in which the philosophical implications of Second International historical materialism were contested. Indispensable for tracing how Bernstein challenged orthodox determinism and how orthodox Marxists (including early Luxemburg) responded.

  POSITION: Documentary collection without a strong thesis, though the introduction leans toward contextualizing revisionism as a legitimate internal development of Marxist thought.
  },
  keywords = {Bernstein, revisionism, Second-International, Kautsky, Luxemburg, historical-materialism, High},
  abstract = {Philip Kain’sMarx, Revolution and Social Democracypresents the case for a social-democratic reading of Marx. The central intention of the book is to convince the reader that market socialism is consistent with a democratic interpretation of Marx. Furthermore, Kain argues, a parliamentary democratic Marx requires neither a specifically socialist state nor public ownership of the means of production. Kain criticizes the Leninist vision of political revolution as a punctual transformation, resulting in state socialism, nationalized economies, centralized planning and so forth, as an authoritarian misrepresentation of Marx (66–75). Instead, Kain proposes, socialism—now understood as democratic planning that frames market regulation, welfare measures, majoritarian democracy and social rights—has been advancing throughout the twentieth century. He argues for seeing socialism as the result of a democratically-instigated, majority-led process of socialization rather than a political convulsion (79–80). Kain then reassures readers that this makes it possible to grasp what looked to Leninists like meliorism as the historical transformation of capitalism into socialism. Against the Leninist vision, Kain proposes market regulation and social measures controlled by a social-democratic parliamentary majority, which aim at reducing inequality and eliminating alienation. Rudolf Meidner’s plan for Swedish Social Democracy from the 1970s is presented in this context as an example of the social democratic transition to market socialism (132–135).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Tonally speaking, the book seems aimed at reassuring readerships interested in Marx as a philosophical figure, but wary of socialism because of the failure of historical communism. Despite strong engagement with both Marx and Engels, the book is not primarily a scholarly intervention in the Marxological field. Too many important debates—the connection between alienation, exploitation and domination; the definition of modes of production in terms of economic exploitation; participatory democracy versus parliamentary representation; and the notion of the elimination of the commodity form through collective ownership of the means of production, for instance—are dealt with through selective quotation, rather than detailed evaluation of the contending interpretations. Nor is there a searching critique of the historical results of actually existing social democracy, such as might be expected in countries where such traditions have held office for prolonged periods. Instead, the book addresses pro-socialist neophytes with the case for seeing social democratic politics as representing the authentic Marxian impulse. It promises them that this rules out the authoritarian communism that has strongly discredited the socialist project. Much of its argument is conducted in non-technical language, appropriate to making contact with philosophical and political novices.

As a rehabilitation of the very idea of taking Marx seriously, for an addressee who may be assumed to be mainly located in North America, the book does a very good job. However, there are some points where it might have done a better job by straightforwardly disagreeing with Marx, rather than prematurely smoothing out difficulties with the social democratic position in the interests of claiming the authority of the master thinker. After all, Marx is on the side of socialists and workers, not the other way around. And socialists today deserve the opportunity to consider Marx’s views in all of their complexity, irrespective of whether they agree with Lenin or Karl Kautsky. Indeed, it is perfectly possible that some ideas, such as the “withering away of the state” and distribution of abundance based on need, are orthogonal to reality. Market socialism and democratic parliamentarism may, or may not, be excellent answers to shortcomings of Marx’s nineteenth-century anticipation of twentieth-century experiments. But to find these rejoinders in the text without an extended discussion of why very serious interpreters have found just the opposite can sometimes seem unnecessarily apologetic, or proselytizing. This is especially the case when the position in question—similar in many respects to those of Kautsky—has been debated in considerable detail, in the context of historical efforts at political implementation.

The most contentious position—because systematically foundational to the entire interpretation—concerns the nature of alienation and its links to exploitation. According to Kain, the foundation for Marx’s critique of capitalism is his interpretation of alienation through commodity fetishism. He proposes that the whole point of socialism is to end alienation, grasped under capitalism as commodity fetishism, and the question would be, whether or not ending alienation is compatible with the persistence of markets. Kain builds up his answer in steps. First, he cites Marx to the effect that in commodity exchange, “a social relation of production appears as something existing apart from human beings, and the distinctive [social] relations. . .as properties of things” (19–20). He glosses this as the idea that:

If workers are alienated from their product, if it is lost to them, if it takes on a life of its own, if it becomes a power over them, it will come to dominate them. This is fetishism. (22)

Here, Kain notices that the “loss” in question happens because the capitalist appropriates the product legally by virtue of property arrangements. This means that alienation and exploitation are the same thing from two different perspectives (22). The clear implication is that ending alienation requires abolishing exploitation, which would have to involve the elimination of private property in the means of production (i.e., the property arrangements that cause commodity fetishism). This is not, however, the conclusion that Kain draws.

Instead, Kain argues that alienation can be eliminated while retaining private property in the means of production. This argument depends on an elision that the non-technical use of the term “market” seems to permit. In popular discourse, no distinction is made between “market,” meaning monetized commodity exchange between independent owners, and “market,” meaning the “market society,” a society characterised by private property in the means of production under conditions of capital accumulation. There can be markets (monetized exchange) without capital accumulation—this point is made in theGrundrisse,for instance. But it does not follow from this that Marxists are indifferent to the question of the property foundation on which market exchange happens. Kain tends to be ambiguous about this point. He references, but does not cite, Marx inCapital, who claims that fetishism-free social production might involve:

Kain argues (I think correctly) that this represents the situation during the transition to socialism, where distribution in proportion to labour time is mediated by money as a representation of value. It is not yet the fully socialist society where money has been replaced by labour certificates and the state by a voluntary association (24). (I note, though Kain does not, that there is no exploitation here because instead of the capitalist owner effectively deducting from each worker’s labour time to make a profit, these are “jointly owned means of production,” with no exploiter present, and therefore no deduction made—according to Marx.) This, Kain asserts, is a market socialism, because it is a system involving monetized exchange. Next, Kain poses the rhetorical question, “if market exchange per se does not cause fetishism, then what is it about market exchange that does?” He answers:},
}

@incollection{giordani2018labriola,
  author = {Giordani, Tommaso},
  title = {Redefining Historical Materialism in the Peripheries of {Marxism}: {Georges Sorel} and {Antonio Labriola} between {France}, {Italy}, and {Germany}},
  booktitle = {Decentering European Intellectual Space},
  year = {2018},
  pages = {88--113},
  publisher = {Brill},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004364530_006},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Giordani argues that both Labriola and Sorel developed versions of historical materialism in conscious opposition to the German Second International orthodoxy. Labriola insisted on the specificity and irreducibility of historical materialism as a method, resisting both positivist naturalization (Kautsky, Plekhanov) and neo-Kantian ethics (Bernstein). Sorel moved from Labriola's influence toward a voluntarist revision that anticipated syndicalism. Together they illustrate how peripheral positions in the socialist movement generated philosophically sophisticated alternatives to orthodox determinism.

  RELEVANCE: Fills a gap by covering Italian and French variants of historical materialism that engaged with the Second International but developed outside its German center. Labriola's formulation is relevant as a direct interlocutor with Engels and as an alternative to both Kautsky's evolutionism and Bernstein's neo-Kantianism.

  POSITION: Peripheral but philosophically significant re-formulations of historical materialism that challenge both orthodox determinism and revisionist ethics.
  },
  keywords = {Labriola, Sorel, Italian-Marxism, historical-materialism, Second-International, periphery, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{lukacs1971history,
  author = {Luk{\'a}cs, Georg},
  title = {History and Class Consciousness: Studies in Marxist Dialectics},
  year = {1971},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lukacs argues that reification -- the transformation
  of social relations into apparently objective, law-governed things
  -- is the central phenomenon of capitalist modernity and the
  primary obstacle to revolutionary consciousness. The proletariat,
  as the class whose labour is most completely commodified, is in
  principle the identical subject-object of history: by achieving
  class consciousness through the mediation of the party, it grasps
  the social totality and thereby overcomes reification.

  RELEVANCE: The founding text of Western Marxist philosophy of
  history. Every subsequent interpretation of Marx's theory of
  history within this tradition either builds on or argues against
  the categories of totality, reification, and class consciousness
  as Lukacs deploys them. The book's conception of history as a
  structured whole knowable from a privileged class standpoint is
  directly at issue in debates about whether Marx's philosophy of
  history is epistemically credible.

  POSITION: Praxis-totality strand of Western Marxism; Hegelian
  reading of historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {Western-Marxism, reification, totality, class-consciousness, historical-materialism, High},
  abstract = {In a recentLondon Review of Books(10/21/04),Jerry Fodor remarks that few outside the profession find current analytic philosophy, in contrast to continental philosophy, at all attractive.The latter is harder to read and requires lots more background, but its popularity seems vastly higher than that of analytic philosophy.Fodor thinks this lack of appeal may be due to our extensive concern with possible worlds and modal ambiguities.But, equally, few lay persons are interested in the fairly prevalent claims that there are really no qualia or that, for example, pain is a myth.Many think that these claims are obviously all wrong, and that they show philosophy is rightly regarded as intellectually impoverished.Consciousness and its penalties are, after all, at the heart of our self-conception, as Livingston stresses inPhilosophical History and the Problem of Consciousness(PHPC).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Analytic philosophy's problems are problems with language.Is there a deep sense in which analytic philosophy's treatment of consciousness involves a serious misconstruction of language?Wittgenstein argued for the view that philosophy's failures in general are often attributable to mistakes about language, broadly construed, and he was preceded in this view by Moore and followed by others, including Ryle and Austin.

While Wittgenstein's approach in works after theTractatuswas typically to focus on specific issues,PHPClooks for a very general problem to explain whatmaybe current analytic philosophy of mind's genuinely world-class failure, the failure to "explain consciousness," as Livingston puts it.

The diagnosis offered is that analytic philosophy has a conception of meaning and explanation which it mistakenly regards as universally correct.The conception is what Livingston terms the "structuralist conception."A central task of the book is to articulate this conception and to trace the development that has placed it so deeply in the minds of analytic philosophers that it is hard for anyone to discern an alternative.Livingston's work combines the history of analytic philosophy with concerns and discussions heavily influenced by a continental sympathy.This sort of synthesis, foreign as it may now seem, is potentially a rich source of interesting ideas.It may also, less fortunately, lead to the sentences of a sort analytic philosophers are often taught -- not always successfully -- to avoid.One may come to feel thatPHPChas too many sentences with sixty or more words.

Does Livingston's project succeed? Some of the most impressive parts of the book are the historically based descriptions and diagnosis of contemporary structuralism, the theory of meaning and explanation that causes the apparent failure of philosophy.Livingston somewhat contentiously begins with the Vienna Circle, whose concerns with mind and knowledge he captures as a prolonged and engaging dialectic.This is contentious because one may also see in Anglo-American philosophy of mind very distinctive Humean approaches to the sort of causal analyses Livingston discuss; equally, others have found functionalism, another concern of Livingston's, in Aristotle.Nonetheless, the scope of the study is large and includes, amongmanyothers, Carnap, Neurath, Smart, Fodor and Chalmers.They are seen as working to elaborate and refine a preoccupation with structure that overall subverts the goal of explaining conscious experience.

In Livingston's story, structuralism becomes an increasingly formal demand on theory."Astructuralorstructuralistexplanation…is one that accounts for particular items bylocatingthem in a broader structure ofrelationsof one kind or another" (3).Structuralists operate with a conception of structured explanation; particular explanations are instances of this structure.Meaning or understanding is elucidated or achieved by generating such explanations.The trap it poses will vary with different types of structures, but the general problem can be illustrated with an example:For physicalists the distinctive structure will be the structure of explanations suitable for objective phenomena such as the sciences treat; but arguably many features of subjective experience -- its immediacy and directness, for example (9) -- will not be explained in such structures.Hence, consciousness arguably must be irremediably problematized by physicalism.

The news about the analytic tradition is not entirely negative, and some of Livingston's most interesting ideas come as delineations of what we can learn from the tradition, and what can be learned from "the method of analytic reflection."Some of the lessons he takes himself to have learned will not be accepted by all in the tradition.For example, the idea that all objective empirical claims are "responsive and responsible" to claims about "our own" conscious experience is a very familiar thought but also both an unclear and a contestable thesis.Similarly, the idea that there are phenomenological laws which are not best thought of as completely factual is based on the Husserl's problematic view that empirical investigations cannot yield knowledge of essences.Nonetheless, philosophers of mind should be concerned with questions about whether experience has a discoverable law-governed structure, and, accordingly, Livingston manages at the very least to raise important questions about the nature of the philosophical project so many take themselves to be engaged upon.

What serious questions about the main thesis of the book might one have?Livingston continuously ascribes to analytic philosophers a theory of meaning that seems to be almost entirely formal; meaning is revealed by logical structure.It is true that even the stress on logical structure is seen as allowing for relations among concepts, and so the theory of meaning is not entirely formal.However, there are very large areas of discussion in philosophy of mind that Livingston does not directly address.He leaves the reader in effect having to interpret all the standardly expressed concerns about ontology and causal closure into his diagnostic schema about structuralism.At many points, arguments that I had learned from, e.g., my old tutor Gilbert Ryle (asignificant figure in the book) as fundamentally motivated by ontology turn out to be, in Livingston's hands, based on apparently very different concerns about logical form.The non-analytic reader may be well prepared to believe that a preoccupation with logical form is at the heart of analytic philosophy of mind's problems with consciousness, but to an analytic reader it may well seem far from clearly true.Hence, even Livingston's historical reading provides interpretations which are not obviously at the very heart of the matter.},
}

@book{korsch1970marxism,
  author = {Korsch, Karl},
  title = {Marxism and Philosophy},
  year = {1970},
  publisher = {NLB},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Korsch contends that orthodox Marxism of the
  Second International committed a fatally undialectical error by
  treating philosophy as simply superseded by science. Philosophy --
  and specifically the Hegelian dialectic -- retains a determinate
  function within the class struggle: Marxism itself must be
  understood as a theoretical practice that stands in dialectical
  relation to revolutionary praxis, not as a positive science
  standing above it.

  RELEVANCE: Together with Lukacs's HCC, this text established the
  foundational claim of Western Marxism that historical materialism
  is not a deterministic science but a reflexive philosophy of praxis
  concerned with consciousness and practice. The diagnosis of a
  crisis of Marxism attributable to positivist deformation bears
  directly on interpretations of Marx's own philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Founding text of the Western Marxist critique of
  economic determinism; parallel to Lukacs in insisting on the
  philosophical dimension of Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Western-Marxism, philosophy, Hegel, praxis, historical-materialism, High},
  abstract = {In 2019, Jürgen Habermas published with Suhrkamp Press his 1700-plus pageAuch eine Geschichte der Philosophie. The work appeared in two volumes, subtitled: (1)The Occidental Constellation of Faith and Knowledgeand (2)Rational Freedom: Traces of Discourses on Faith and Reason. In the English language,Also a History of Philosophy(AHP), masterfully translated by Ciarin Cronin, is published by Polity Press in three volumes: (1)The Project of Genealogy of Postmetaphysical Thinking(2022); (2)The Occidental Constellation of Faith and Knowledge(2024); and (3)Rational Freedom. Traces of the Discourse on Faith and Knowledge(2025).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

The review that follows focuses on Volume III of the English translation. First, I provide an overview of some of the work’s more important claims. Then I pose some questions, principally about the work’s systematic philosophic claims but also, briefly, about its status as a history of philosophy.

As the title indicates, the work is a history of philosophy, one that traces the relationship of faith and reason as it played out historically, primarily in Western thought. As the title also implies, however, the work is more than just a history of philosophy, distinctive though that itself is. It is also and perhaps most significantly a defense of the notion of postmetaphysical reason, which has been a central focus of Habermas’s work for the past thirty years or so. AHP is distinctive, however, in that it presents that defense via a genealogical reconstruction of the evolution of philosophical thought.

Central to this “genealogy of postmetaphysical thinking” is an effort to redeem and recast for contemporary thought crucial insights and assumptions introduced to human civilization during what, following Karl Jaspers, Habermas calls the Axial Age. Occurring in the period between 800 and 200 BCE, the Axial Age reflected the ascendance of the major world religions, including Confucianism, Taoism, Hinduism, Buddhism, and Judaism, as well as classical Greek philosophy. Common to all these movements was an affirmation of principles and values central to the subsequent development principally of Western thought and culture. Among others, these included the idea of objective validity, the principle of the worth and equality of all individuals, a commitment to the welfare of humankind, and an expectation that human affairs be governed by binding norms and impartial standards of justice and morality.

Over 1700 pages, Habermas presents the ensuing interplay of faith and reason as a historical “learning process”, in which the principles and values bequeathed to humanity by the Axial Age are further clarified while gradually purged of the metaphysical and theological detritus that has impeded their proper articulation. Resulting from this process of learning are core elements of Habermas’s idea of postmetaphysical thinking. At issue, in particular, is a notion of rationality committed to norms of objective validity, impartial truth, human equality and historical progress, where those norms are deemed to express, not an absolute authority to which humans are vertically related, but the immanent, horizontally directed, open-ended and fallibilistically conceived practices through which historically situated and “communicatively socialized” individuals shape and reshape the conditions of their shared existence.

Volume III is itself distinctive, as here the genealogy first details the historical emergence of postmetaphysical thinking. Habermas’s focus is on Hume and Kant, each of whom advances a philosophy that rejects the metaphysical assumptions that had accompanied the preceding discourse on faith and knowledge. They do so in diverse ways, however. Hume does so by dismissing or “deconstructing” the religious-theological legacy, while Kant reappropriates or “reconstructs” it. Habermas’s preference is decidedly for the Kantian option.

Indeed, the task of Volume III, as its subtitle indicates, is to clarify for postmetaphysical thinking the concept of “rational freedom” (vernünftige Freiheit) initially elaborated by Kant. That Habermas nonetheless focuses on Hume is attributable not only to the fact that Kant develops his position in response to that of Hume but also that the latter underwrites the scientistic naturalism that today has spawned the self-regulating social systems against which, in the text, he seeks to “encourage” the idea of rational freedom.

Habermas understands rational freedom specifically in terms of Kant’s idea of practical self-legislation. He dubs this idea “one of the few major innovations in the history philosophy” (262), yet presents it, consistent with the effort to discern “traces of the discourse on faith and knowledge”, as rearticulating assumptions that have long informed religious traditions. Thus, while a comprehensive understanding of moral obligation was historically construed in terms of divinely ordained commands, Kant’s understanding of it appeals to universal duties that are humanly self-imposed. Similarly, while Kant continues to espouse the notion, central also to religions, that thought might provide human beings with holistic world orientation, he does so, not by seeking to integrate individuals into a preexisting order of being, but by referencing a world order reflective of the constitutive activity of human beings themselves.},
}

@book{gramsci1971selections,
  author = {Gramsci, Antonio},
  title = {Selections from the Prison Notebooks},
  year = {1971},
  publisher = {International Publishers},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gramsci reconceives historical materialism as a
  philosophy of praxis centered on hegemony: durable political power
  requires not just coercion but intellectual and moral leadership
  that permeates civil society, reshaping common sense. The
  base/superstructure model is replaced by a more fluid conception
  in which economic crisis becomes politically transformative only
  when mediated by ideological and organizational work. History is
  made by collective will forged through the cathartic translation
  of economic interests into ethical and political projects.

  RELEVANCE: Gramsci's concept of hegemony is the most influential
  Western Marxist intervention into the question of historical agency
  and the superstructures' relative autonomy. It directly challenges
  both economic determinism and Lukacs's privileging of proletarian
  class consciousness, proposing instead a processual account of how
  historically specific power blocs are assembled and contested.

  POSITION: Gramscian strand; absolute historicism and philosophy
  of praxis as alternative to base/superstructure determinism.
  },
  keywords = {Gramsci, hegemony, philosophy-of-praxis, historical-materialism, civil-society, High},
  abstract = {This is the tenth volume by Nietzsche in theCambridge Texts in the History of Philosophyseries, giving him the most books in the series (followed by Kant with seven volumes). Like all the other Nietzsche volumes,Writings from the Early Notebookscontains an Introduction followed by suggestions for further reading, together with bibliographical notes on the texts and notes on the translation. The texts are presented in a new translation. Added to them is an impressive number of very informative footnotes that provide the biographical, historical and intellectual background without which a lot of the material would be barely comprehensible. All these features make this volume, like many others in the series, very helpful for both students and researchers.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

As the title of the volume suggests, the texts it contains are a selection. They come from the vast amount of notes — almost half of the historical critical edition of his works is filled with them — that Nietzsche wrote down throughout his life in many notebooks. Some of these notes are just a couple of words whereas others are of considerable length, sometimes filling several pages. They contain remarks and reflections on topics that aroused his intellectual curiosity, covering just about every conceivable field of human life ranging from contemporary politics and cultural history to art and aesthetic phenomena and above all to almost all domains of philosophy both theoretical and practical. The roughly 240 pages of notes in the volume are from the period between 1869 and 1879. That they represent a very exclusive selection is documented by the fact that the notes from this period in the historical critical edition cover around 1400 pages. This raises a question about the principles and the criteria of selection. Unfortunately, neither the editors nor the translator explicitly address this question. However, I have the impression that the guiding thread for the selection was the general relevance of the material selected for what Alexander Nehamas in his introduction tells us about Nietzsche’s preoccupations and main interests during that period. This impression is not just based on the notes chosen. It also suggests itself indirectly because of the inclusion of three texts discussed quite thoroughly in the introduction, two of which are normally not considered to be notes but fall under the rubric of unpublished writings (On the Pathos of TruthandOn Truth and Lie in an Extra-Moral Sense), while the third (On Schopenhauer) is from an earlier period. However that may be, the resulting collection is very convincing and informative.

In the introduction, Nehamas (who has done more than anyone else in the English-speaking world to secure Nietzsche his due place in the chain of serious philosophers) approaches the notes by identifying the overriding themes that occupied Nietzsche during the period. He then goes on “to discuss a few specific issues relevant both to Nietzsche’s notes and to his published works in order to indicate the various ways in which each kind of writing can cast light on the other” (XIX). This strategy brings some order into the material and is very convincing. Nehamas identifies Nietzsche’s dominant themes during the period as: “the philosophy of Schopenhauer, the music of Richard Wagner, and the importance of ancient Greek art and civilization for a renaissance of German culture” (XIV). There is no question that these three were indeed the dominant themes in the 70’s, and they keep resonating in one way or another, and with different intensity, in all his writings and notes. The value of Nehamas’ decision to focus on what is relevant to the published writings can also be seen from the fact that examining the notes with an eye merely towards what is mentioned in them most often would likely result in a list of disconnected subjects such as the philosophy of Kant, the Pre-Socratics (especially Heraclitus and Empedocles) and science and topics such as knowledge, truth and history. Such a list might be useful and even informative, but it cannot provide an introduction to the context in which the notes were written. It is such a context the reader of the notes needs; Nehamas is well aware of this.

However, a small question remains: even if we follows Nehamas’ hermeneutical maxim and uses Nietzsche’s published work from the 70’s as a measuring stick, we might be tempted to doubt the claim that Schopenhauer, Wagner and Greek art and civilization are the topics most pressing for Nietzsche during that period. Itis hard to avoid feeling that there is a rupture both in style and thematic focus between Nietzsche’s published writings of the first half of the 70’s and those of the second half, i.e. betweenThe Birth of Tragedy(1872) and theUntimely Meditations(1873-1876) on the one hand andHuman, All Too Human(1878) on the other. WhereasThe Birth of Tragedyand theUntimely Meditationsare indeed outstanding witnesses of Nietzsche’s preoccupation with Schopenhauer, Wagner and Greek art, we might be less inclined to think of these topics as the organizing forces behindHuman, All Too Human. These observations are not meant to be critical of Nehamas’ suggestions — on the contrary, I take them to be more plausible than any alternative could be. Rather they are intended to point to a more general question concerning Nietzsche-studies: does it really do justice to Nietzsche’s way of thinking to give his thematically scattered reflections some kind of unity by providing a topical framework for them? Maybe there is no unity and the notes reflect just this.

A similar question can be posed about how to approach an assessment of Nietzsche’s discussion of issues like truth or philosophy or art as well. In other words: what are the criteria of assessment? What is the equivalent to unity we are looking for when we are evaluating Nietzsche’s views? Much of the philosophical literature on Nietzsche pursues what I would call a ‘rational’ reconstruction whichattempts to provide the most reasonable account of Nietzsche’s work. When evaluating, or even just describing, Nietzsche’s positions, what basis do we have for expecting him to have a high regard for, or at least to adhere to, the standard norms of rational discourse, i.e. to norms like consistency, definiteness or awareness of consequences? Many commentators do their best to convince us that there is a reasonable or rational story to be told about the issues Nietzsche deals with, thus in some way insinuating that Nietzsche can be subjected to the standard norms mentioned. In doing so we could even appeal to Nietzsche as a witness for this way of proceeding because when discussing the work of other people he himself treats them as at least implicitly committed to these norms. This is shown quite nicely in his treatment of Schopenhauer as documented in the small textOn Schopenhauercontained in this volume. Here Nietzsche criticizes Schopenhauer in an almost ‘professional’ way by pointing out inconsistencies, conceptual ambiguities and fallacious inferences in his attempt to establish the will as a thing in itself and to defend the principle of individuation. This Nietzschean attitude to the texts of others can be seen as an invitation to or even as a demand for dealing with his texts in the same ‘rational’ manner.

But somehow this does not seem to be right because such an approach tends to make Nietzsche’s remarks and observations fairly tedious. Take, for example, two topics Nehamas discusses in his introduction: history and truth. Concerning history,we can find many illuminating outlines of the three ways Nietzsche says we can use to approach historyand of his criticism of the ‘modern’ attempt to tackle history in a ‘scientific spirit’ because ‘scientific history’ has the tendency to wipe out human creativity.There is no doubt that this indeed is Nietzsche’s message. But this ‘discursive’ or ‘rational’ mode of presenting Nietzsche’s stance on history is in danger of giving his view a rather common-sensical, maybe even uninteresting, touch. Missing in this mode of presentation is the communication of that sense of urgency or of immediate uneasiness that seems to have been a driving force behind Nietzsche’s engagement with history as a major issue. I take it that it is this unsaid background that plays a decisive role in awarding Nietzsche’s material claims the status of important and interesting insights (if one is intrigued by Nietzsche’s texts at all). Nehamas addresses this background in his introduction, thus opening the way to a more complete understanding.

A structurally similar objection could be raised against a ‘rational’ reading of Nietzsche’s remarks on truth even if it is well informed. According to such a reading, we cannot avoid coming to the conclusion that Nietzsche’s view on truth is deeply flawed either because it denies truth altogether, which cannot be done without self-contradiction, or because it is based on, let us say, his bad arguments against correspondence. At the same time we have the feeling that such a criticism somehow misses the mark mainly because we are inclined to think that the mistakes attributed to Nietzsche in this context are so elementary and obvious that we would have to conjure up special reasons in order to ascribe them to him. After all, Nietzsche does not seem to have been an unqualified idiot. Thus there remains that sense of perplexity and confusion which refuses to acknowledge that the normal ‘rational’ reading accurately captures what Nietzsche was after in thinking about truth. Though Nehamas is well aware of the problems of Nietzsche’s remarks on truth, his introductory comments show that at the same time he is sensitive to, and insists on, the motivational and psychological environment in which Nietzsche’s statements are embedded, thus recognizing that the picture is much more complex.

If there really are these limitations to what I have called ‘rational’ readings of Nietzsche’s texts and if these limitations indeed encourage the suspicion that in the end Nietzsche’s claims are bound to be either trivial, common-sense, or rather silly errors, then one can recommend Nehamas’ approach to Nietzsche as a relieving antidote. Does this make him an ‘irrational’ commentator? If this term is understood to hint at the need for a richer reading, a reading that integrates more ‘perspectives’ than those available to rational reconstruction, then an ‘irrational’ interpretation might be promising. How such an interpretation might look one learns best from all of Nehamas’ work on Nietzsche (and on others as well).},
}

@book{marcuse1941reason,
  author = {Marcuse, Herbert},
  title = {Reason and Revolution: Hegel and the Rise of Social Theory},
  year = {1941},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marcuse argues that Hegel's dialectical method,
  properly understood, is inherently critical and anti-positivist:
  reason is not reconciled with the given social order but stands
  over against it as a normative standard. The transition from Hegel
  to Marx is not a materialist inversion of idealism but a
  radicalization of Hegel's own critique of immediacy; social theory
  inherits Hegel's insight that actuality must be measured against
  rational possibility.

  RELEVANCE: This is the Frankfurt School's foundational
  reconstruction of the Hegel-Marx relationship as one of
  continuation and radicalization rather than rupture. It establishes
  the methodological basis for a critical theory of history that
  opposes both idealism and vulgar materialism and insists on the
  immanent standard of reason against facticity.

  POSITION: Frankfurt School; critical theory reading of the
  Hegel-Marx relationship as basis for a non-positivist philosophy
  of history.
  },
  keywords = {Marcuse, Hegel, critical-theory, Frankfurt-School, reason, High},
  abstract = {To preserve the unrestricted authority of reason in the moral sphere, Kant argued that the scientific use of reason yields knowledge of appearances only, not of things as they are in themselves. This trade-off sparked both consternation and controversy. Among Kant’s contemporaries, the most prominent—J. G. Fichte—set the terms for “German Idealism”, the project of constructing a consistent rational system on the basis of Kant’s “Copernican Turn”. In the second half of the nineteenth century, neo-Kantian philosophers pushed back against this idealism—as did Dilthey’s hermeneutics and the Brentano-inspired phenomenological movement—without fully escaping its pull. Underlying these developments, as G. Anthony Bruno shows in his splendid new book, was anxiety over the “fate of reason”.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

The past fifty years have seen an efflorescence of studies that interpret the yield of these post-Kantian takes on a “science of intelligibility” (2), and Bruno’s philosophical-historical reconstruction of the path from Kant to Heidegger engages extensively with that literature. However, his choice of Fichte’s neologism, “facticity”, asfils conducteuris quite original. Facticity is not equivalent to empirical facts. As expressed in propositions, facts have a rational structure; “facticity”, in contrast, is a portmanteau for any element in a philosophical system that appears as a “brute” given or rationally ungrounded posit. Examples include Kant’s “rhapsodic” Metaphysical Deduction of the categories, and his undemonstrated claim that the faculties of sensibility and understanding aretoto caelodistinct (22, 58).

While the German Idealists found such brute givens unacceptable, difficulties in eliminating them nudged later philosophers toward accepting that reason’s reach is limited. Bruno sees Schelling’s late “positive” philosophy as the turning point, and he subsequently provides illuminating chapters on Emil Lask’s alethiology, Wilhelm Dilthey’s hermeneutics, and Martin Heidegger’s phenomenology. Thus, the fate of reason—from Kant’s appeal to “the primacy of the practical” to Heidegger’s embrace of the “primacy of the factical” (92)—confronts us with a decision: either continue the search for ascienceof intelligibility, as did Husserl (92-96), or accept that intelligibility is not rationally grounded but ever remains a matter for interpretive understanding.

Bruno embraces the latter. Though philosophy canidentifythe factical conditions that enable our meaningful engagement with things, this does not yield a rationalLetztbegründung; we can only make sense of things piecemeal and from within. Philosophy occupies a “unique modal space” where it discerns “conditions of intelligibility whose necessity is not logical yet whose contingency is not empirical” (3). For instance, in recognizing that “thrownness” contributes to making Dasein’s understanding of being possible, Heidegger identifies a condition on intelligibility that is “neither logically necessary, since [its] denial is thinkable, nor empirically contingent, since [it] characterizes Dasein’s very being” (219). With Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel, we can entertain thethoughtof rationally grounding such a condition, but doing so “exhibits a kind of inauthenticity”, a “dogmatic slumber of sorts” (3).

The argument supporting this conclusion proceeds in three parts, devoted to three “crises of method” in the historical progression from Kant to Heidegger.

The first concerns Kant’s “transcendental” logic, which locates the ground of empirical knowledge in the transcendental unity of apperception, the “I”. Because the I is notgiven in intuition, it is a cognitively dubious concept whose ambiguity lends itself to conflicting interpretations. To some, the Critical Philosophy represented thoroughgoing skepticism; others complained that it could not refute “Spinozism”, meaning the “atheistic” or “nihilistic” possibility that the ultimate principle is not the I (freedom) but nature (necessity)(33, 80). Bruno carefully explains these worries and provides an illuminating account of Fichte’s initial attempt to address them by appeal to the I’s practical consciousness of freedom, an “intellectual intuition” of itself as both act and fact:Thathandlung(51). When this proved insufficient, Fichte attempted a “genetic deduction” of the “oneness of fact and act”, but Hegel deemed this a merely “subjective” idealism, a judgment that “amounts to a charge of vestigial facticity” (73, 100).

Unfortunately, Hegel’s own efforts to transform Fichte’s dialectic into an “absolute” (fully presuppositionless) logic produced a second crisis of method. For Hegel, neither the subject’s freedom nor the object’s “being” provide an adequate starting point for philosophy. Instead, one must attend to the way one-sided notions of subjectivity and objectivity are negated and “sublated” in experience until one attains to the “speculative concept”, where genuine idealism—a dialectical science of intelligibility no longer misled by the merelyapparentgulf between subjectivity and objectivity—can be pursued (99-153).

Dialectics itself falls into crisis, however, when Schelling—“a pivotal yet neglected figure in the history of the concept of facticity” (7)—detects a differentkindof presupposition in Hegel’s method. Because Hegel’s logic is propelled by “negation”, it must presuppose the “reality that [it] is meant to rationalize”, namely, “theexistencethat contributes to intelligibility” (178). Mirroring themetaphysicalquestion of why is there something rather than nothing, Schelling asks, “Why is there meaning at all, why is there not meaninglessness instead?” (182). On Schelling’s approach, then, as Bruno astutely notes, “intelligibility demands not just the deduction of the whatness of things but also thepresuppositionof thethatnessof things” (198), thereby locating facticity in an “existential register” (214). As Schelling argues inDie Weltalter, rationalizingthatpresupposition “is never complete” because it “is coextensive with the whole of history” (209).},
}

